# .containerignore — build context for the upgrade-acceptance image
# (upgradeacceptance/container/Containerfile).
#
# The image builds from the repo root so the driver sees the whole module
# and its git history (artifact N is built from an earlier revision), but
# the context must not carry the working tree's byproducts: per-session
# state, caches, editor droppings, and build outputs. Two worktree facts
# make this load-bearing rather than hygiene:
#
#   - In a linked worktree `.git` is a pointer file whose target lives
#     outside the context, so without this exclusion the image's copy of
#     the history — the tree artifact N is built from — may not resolve.
#   - The driver builds N with `git archive` from the image's own `.git`.
#     An image built from a worktree must therefore carry a full clone:
#     see the Containerfile's worktree repair below.
#
# The engine reads `.containerignore` (and `.dockerignore`) at the context
# root — not beside the Containerfile — for `-f` builds; verified against
# the local engine (an ignore file beside the Containerfile is silently
# not read).

# Per-session and per-worktree state: never source, and in a linked
# worktree `.git` is a pointer outside the context.
.git
.agent/
.pi/
.pi-*/
.donmai/
.claude/

# Caches and build outputs.
.cache/
**/.gocache/
**/go-build/
**/.golangci-lint-cache/
**/.golangci-cache/
bin/
dist/
coverage.out
acceptance-results/

# Editor and OS droppings.
.vscode/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Secrets by accident, never by design.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
!.env.example
